# LINE ENDINGS ARE A DEPLOYMENT CONCERN HERE, NOT A STYLE PREFERENCE.
#
# This repository is authored on Windows and every artefact it produces runs on Linux — inside
# the agent image, inside docker-mailserver, inside a kind pod. Without this file, git stored
# CRLF and a shell script arrived on the mail host with `\r` on the end of its shebang. The
# kernel then looks for an interpreter literally named "/bin/bash\r", does not find it, and the
# script silently does not run: the mail server came up UNHARDENED, with submission accepting
# unauthenticated senders, and nothing reported an error. Found on hp-tiger by the infra agent,
# who had to work around it by hand.
#
# That failure mode is why the shell scripts are pinned explicitly rather than left to
# `text=auto`: a config file with a stray CR usually errors loudly, while a script with one
# fails by not existing.

# Normalise everything to LF in the repository. Working-copy endings stay native.
* text=auto

# Files that CROSS THE BOUNDARY to a Linux host, checked out or copied, must be LF in the
# working tree too — several of these are delivered by scp rather than by a git checkout, so
# normalising only the repository would not be enough.
*.sh          text eol=lf
*.py          text eol=lf
*.yml         text eol=lf
*.yaml        text eol=lf
*.php         text eol=lf
Dockerfile    text eol=lf
*.cmd         text eol=crlf

# Not text. Left alone entirely so git never rewrites a byte.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
*.parquet binary
